00:14:55.185 --> 00:14:57.075
that we can fill in a hydrogen here.

00:14:57.075 --> 00:15:01.695
Remember four bonds for every one
of these carbons that we have.

00:15:01.695 --> 00:15:07.335
So let's say if I highlight the carbon
here in green and we can zoom in on that.

00:15:07.845 --> 00:15:10.605
Well, we can count the
number of bonds, right?

00:15:10.610 --> 00:15:16.095
We have 1, 2, 3, 4 bonds
all around that structure.

00:15:16.485 --> 00:15:20.025
So there's a hydrogen there,
so that is an aldehyde.

00:15:20.085 --> 00:15:21.825
Now, what else is there in the structure?

00:15:21.825 --> 00:15:25.425
There might be one other, you might
see your big aromatic group as well,

00:15:25.815 --> 00:15:28.005
which is the ring structure here.

00:15:28.395 --> 00:15:33.375
So that is your phenyl group, which
is a part of your ring as well.

00:15:33.465 --> 00:15:34.935
So those are all the different types.

00:15:35.370 --> 00:15:39.089
Let's go into a common, another common
mistake and problem that students make for

00:15:39.089 --> 00:15:42.599
identifying their functional groups, which
is so important for your organic course.

00:15:42.839 --> 00:15:43.079
Okay?

00:15:43.079 --> 00:15:46.050
This example, once again,
identify the functional groups.

00:15:46.500 --> 00:15:50.010
Now with these, what a lot of students
do is they make common mistakes that

00:15:50.015 --> 00:15:52.170
identify groups that they see right away.

00:15:53.205 --> 00:15:56.865
Make sure you look at everything
connected to it, because it could

00:15:56.895 --> 00:15:58.845
be a slightly different example.

00:15:59.355 --> 00:16:03.915
So for example, right here I see
that, oh, I have an alcohol group

00:16:04.095 --> 00:16:05.235
that is attached to this ring.

00:16:05.235 --> 00:16:07.995
We have an oh, which makes perfect sense.

00:16:08.055 --> 00:16:13.245
However, one thing that you can quickly
notice is that there is a C double

00:16:13.250 --> 00:16:15.315
bond O that is right next to it.

00:16:15.735 --> 00:16:18.705
We have to look at the
entire structure all around.

00:16:19.725 --> 00:16:22.545
This is a much larger group here.

00:16:22.935 --> 00:16:24.945
This is a carboxylic acid.

00:16:25.485 --> 00:16:29.625
Whenever we have c double bonded
o's look on both sides of the

00:16:29.630 --> 00:16:31.245
street before crossing, right?

00:16:31.515 --> 00:16:34.575
So when we have that carbon, we're
gonna look to left, look to the right,

00:16:34.875 --> 00:16:36.165
see if there's anything near it.

00:16:36.525 --> 00:16:38.865
And you should do this for the all
functional groups because they could

00:16:38.895 --> 00:16:41.415
have an altered identity because of that.

00:16:41.415 --> 00:16:43.125
So that is a carboxylic acid.

00:16:43.740 --> 00:16:44.490
What else do we have?

00:16:44.580 --> 00:16:45.240
Over here?

00:16:45.245 --> 00:16:46.860
We see an NH2, right?

00:16:46.860 --> 00:16:48.120
So that is something different.

00:16:48.120 --> 00:16:50.760
Now the question, is it
a mean or is it an aide?

00:16:51.210 --> 00:16:53.340
A mean is the minimum configuration.

00:16:53.760 --> 00:16:57.300
So we only see an NH two connect
to the rest of the chain.

00:16:57.660 --> 00:17:00.240
So this is an amine group.

00:17:01.230 --> 00:17:07.740
Our next portion over on the far
side, we have this over here, which

00:17:07.745 --> 00:17:11.130
is referring to an alcohol, right?

00:17:12.075 --> 00:17:16.274
And then we also once again
have our benzene structure.

00:17:16.815 --> 00:17:19.274
So we have, we highlight the ring here.

00:17:19.274 --> 00:17:25.514
We can see that we have a benzene or an
aromatic, also known as a phenyl group.

00:17:26.685 --> 00:17:28.784
And that is our, is,
that is our last part.

00:17:28.790 --> 00:17:30.585
So we have all those little pieces.

00:17:30.865 --> 00:17:34.455
Let's try another, which you're
more than welcome to pause the

00:17:34.455 --> 00:17:38.175
video and try yourself and see if
you can try to identify all the

00:17:38.175 --> 00:17:40.065
functional groups on this compound.

00:17:41.100 --> 00:17:44.879
Now when we look at this,
the first thing is, is I, I

00:17:44.885 --> 00:17:48.030
notice is this C double bond O.

00:17:48.030 --> 00:17:49.649
That is right here on the
side of the structure.

00:17:51.149 --> 00:17:54.149
Now the thing with the C double
bonded O is trying to figure

00:17:54.149 --> 00:17:56.610
out exactly which one is it.

00:17:57.090 --> 00:18:00.959
Now this, we can see that well,
we're missing one bond here

00:18:00.959 --> 00:18:02.669
and it has a hydrogen there.

00:18:03.060 --> 00:18:05.850
So remember we always
count to four, right?

00:18:06.209 --> 00:18:07.469
How do I see the four?

00:18:07.500 --> 00:18:09.719
Well, we have our hydrogen here.

00:18:10.199 --> 00:18:14.730
We have the single bonded carbon
to carbon bond, and then we have

00:18:14.790 --> 00:18:16.230
one part of the double bond.

00:18:16.709 --> 00:18:18.389
The second part of the double bond, right?

00:18:18.389 --> 00:18:23.250
Those four colors all represent
the four bonds that are here,

00:18:23.610 --> 00:18:25.080
um, in this particular group.

00:18:25.649 --> 00:18:29.879
So this is what is representing
and aldehyde, right?

00:18:29.879 --> 00:18:31.530
So that is our group here.

00:18:32.129 --> 00:18:33.840
So let's label that now.

00:18:33.840 --> 00:18:34.889
What other groups do I have?

00:18:35.580 --> 00:18:39.210
Well, I also have another group
that looks very similar to that.

00:18:39.215 --> 00:18:44.760
I have C double bonded O over here,
but this is an example of a ketone that

00:18:44.760 --> 00:18:46.320
is on the other side of the structure.

00:18:47.010 --> 00:18:49.710
And then the last group is an alcohol.

00:18:50.520 --> 00:18:53.160
Now, last thing is what
a common mistake is.

00:18:53.340 --> 00:18:55.500
Is this a fennel group?

00:18:55.560 --> 00:18:56.400
There's a ring here.

00:18:56.730 --> 00:19:00.180
However, it's missing a key
piece, like the one above.

00:19:00.720 --> 00:19:03.510
One above had those
alternating double bonds.

00:19:03.750 --> 00:19:04.920
This doesn't exist here.

00:19:05.400 --> 00:19:10.680
So this is just a normal ring
or cyclic compound versus the as

00:19:10.680 --> 00:19:13.320
cyclic that we talked about in
part one, which is just a chain.

00:19:13.830 --> 00:19:17.340
So there's different types of
chains and organic hydrocarbons

00:19:17.340 --> 00:19:18.360
that we are going to see.

